            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified the following air leakage (heat loss) locations:

** Some around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")

** The old bathroom exhaust fan on the main floor. It looks like the exhaust fan damper is not sealing well and could be replaced with the new ENERGY STAR* exhaust fan.

** Back door of the main floor (can seal with weather stripping)

** The current  5" chimney and 5" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace

Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 90 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving a minimum 20% reduction qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1,000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R20 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.

Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 1,500 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R10 or R15, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will decrease.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (10 total between the main floor and second floor) are older double pane vinyl windows, that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 210 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6 GJ, and qualify for up to $2,500 in rebates (10 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:

A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:

????????? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)

????????? Intertek Canada

????????? Keystone Certifications

????????? Labtest Certification (LC)

????????? QAI Laboratories

????????? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:

????????? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;

????????? Date of installation;

????????? Window make and model numbers;

????????? NRCan Reference Number;

????????? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;

????????? Number of windows and location of each installation;

????????? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);

????????? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and

????????? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.



Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home, provided only by the exhaust fans in the main floor and second floor bathrooms. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, your energy costs will increase by $ 200 and increase your annual energy usage by 5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 170 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 930 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ,  and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with two older 83% efficient oil furnaces. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (adding more insulation, installing energy efficient windows) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnaces with a 97% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 650 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 42 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. Note: a 97% AFUE condensing furnace upgrade will qualify you for the Canada Greener Homes Grant (see below), however to qualify for a GNWT grant the upgrade furnace must only be a minimum of 96%. The difference in savings between the two furnaces is minimal.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified propane-fired furnace with a minimum requirement of 97% AFUE. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

MOISTURE IN ATTIC:



During my investigation of your attic, I witnessed significant amount of frost buildup blocking the vents, moist insulation and dark stains on the roof boards. These indicates that there is excessive moisture in your attic. 

Your house has had a home energy evaluation in October of 2014, where the attic space &amp; insulation appeared to be dry &amp; no sign of moisture damage. It leads me to believe that the moisture is being introduced in the attic either by wood stove flue which is going through the attic or the second-floor bathroom fan.

I suspect the bathroom fan is not properly installed, hence venting warm, moist air into the attic rather than outside. Flexible bathroom vent pipes tend to have sags that gather water, freeze &amp; split the pipe ????????? perhaps this is happening in your house? 

I would recommend consulting attic/roof specialist who can do extensive investigation to find the exact source of moisture. This is a serious problem that must be addressed. If left untreated, it can damage critical structural elements of the home, such as the trusses and rafters, ruin insulation, and lead to the formation of potentially toxic black mold.  



DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $17,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $2,500 for the windows

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $500 for the air leakage reduction

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system, however this would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $250

     - Programmable Thermostat - 50% up to $150

     - LED Lighting - see section further down

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.25 per litre 

Propane - $1.00

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

